#!/bin/bash
# land-the-stone-has-a-say.sh — per-stone Vedic weights (no more nine-carat
# blue sapphires), and a budget slider that says what your number buys.
#
# BOTH SIDES (together-city-chat + together-city-react). Vercel AND Railway.
# PRECONDITION: land-one-bag.sh.
set -euo pipefail

cd "$(dirname "$0")"
[ -d together-city-chat ] || { echo "!! Run this from the Together-Ai repo root."; exit 1; }

if [ -f .git/index.lock ] && [ ! -s .git/index.lock ]; then
  echo "== clearing a stale empty .git/index.lock"
  rm -f .git/index.lock
fi

NEEDS="The stone has a say in its own weight"
LOG="$(git log --oneline -40)"
case "$LOG" in
  *"$NEEDS"*) echo "== \"$NEEDS\" is already here. Nothing to do."; exit 0 ;;
esac
case "$LOG" in
  *"A bag that forgets"*) ;;
  *) echo "!! Run land-one-bag.sh first — this is written against the tree it produces."; exit 1 ;;
esac

ALLOWED='^ M together-city-react/(public/assets/img/(apple-touch-icon-180|tc-icon-1024|tc-icon-192|tc-icon-512|tc-icon-maskable-512)\.png|public/downloads/TogetherCity\.apk)$'
DIRTY="$(git status --porcelain \
  | grep -Ev '^\?\? (land-.*\.sh|push-.*\.sh|.*\.patch)$' \
  | grep -Ev "$ALLOWED" || true)"
if [ -n "$DIRTY" ]; then
  echo "!! The tree carries changes this script did not expect:"; echo "$DIRTY"; exit 1
fi

PATCH="$(mktemp "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/land.XXXXXX")"
trap 'rm -f "$PATCH"' EXIT
cat <<'B64EOF' | tr -d '\n' | openssl base64 -d -A > "$PATCH"

B64EOF
WANT="d79e603b0223c9e9a253f36280ce329097266c5ab16155a0d2029ff1f234a785"
GOT="$(shasum -a 256 "$PATCH" | awk '{print $1}')"
[ "$GOT" = "$WANT" ] || { echo "!! Patch is corrupt."; echo "   want $WANT"; echo "   got  $GOT"; exit 1; }
echo "== patch verified"
git apply --check -C1 "$PATCH" || { echo "!! Patch does not apply cleanly. Nothing written."; exit 1; }
git apply -C1 "$PATCH"
echo "== applied"

echo "== gates: the API"
cd together-city-chat
npx tsc --noEmit
npx jest src/astrology
cd ..

echo "== gates: the web app"
cd together-city-react
npx tsc --noEmit
npx vitest run
node scripts/a11y-audit.mjs
node scripts/motion-ceiling.mjs
node scripts/lint-ceiling.mjs
npx vite build
cd ..

git add together-city-chat/src/astrology \
        together-city-react/src/features/astrology \
        together-city-react/src/app/one-stone-answer.test.ts
git commit -F - <<'MSG'
The stone has a say in its own weight

IT WAS PRESCRIBING NINE CARATS OF BLUE SAPPHIRE. Between ₹1,35,000 and
₹4,50,000 of Neelam, to a hundred-kilo citizen, on the live site.

The rule it used is real and it is the one most often quoted: about a ratti for
every ten kilos of body weight, at the trade's 0.91 carat to the ratti. Quoted
alone it is a rule about the WEARER with nothing in it about the STONE, and blue
sapphire is the exact stone practice is most careful about — worn small, three
to five ratti, with heavy Neelam as the classic warning. A diamond makes the
same point from the other end: nobody is prescribed nine carats of it, Venus's
stone is worn under two ratti, and the price is why the tradition ever had to
say so.

So each of the nine now carries its own customary range and the body-weight rule
places somebody INSIDE it rather than setting the figure outright. Coral and
hessonite are worn heavy, sapphire and diamond light, and a rule that gave them
the same weight was ignoring the thing every practitioner is most careful about.
Outside a stone's range the wearer sits at whichever end they are nearest, and
the sheet says which — "this is the top of it; the general rule would go higher
and this stone is not worn that heavy" is a recommendation, where a bare number
is not.

A substitute is still worn heavier, but the factor now scales the RANGE rather
than the answer, so an upratna is bounded by what its own stone is worn at.

The same citizen: 9 ct becomes 4.5, and ₹1,35,000–₹4,50,000 becomes
₹67,500–₹2,25,000 — with a ₹1,650 amethyst standing beside it as a real
alternative rather than a footnote.

AND A BUDGET SLIDER AT THE FOOT OF THE PAGE. Four sheets, each honestly priced,
still left the citizen doing arithmetic across them to answer the only question
they actually have. A blue sapphire at ₹67,500 and an amethyst standing in for
it at ₹1,650 are both correct answers to the same chart; which one is THEIRS
depends on a number only they know.

The rank is the spending order — it already says which stone to buy first, so it
also says which one the money reaches first. The recommended stone is taken
whenever it fits and a stand-in only when it does not, named as a stand-in
rather than quietly swapped in. What it could not afford carries the figure it
was short by, because a gap with a number on it is a decision and a gap without
one is a blank.

That budget is NOT saved, and the difference matters: the Beauty hub's is a
standing monthly limit the engine plans against, and this is somebody moving a
slider to see what a figure buys. Storing it would turn an idle question into a
commitment nobody made.
MSG

echo "== committed"
cat <<'DONE'

===============================================================
 Landed: The stone has a say in its own weight
 Both sides — push, then give Railway a minute before opening
 Astrology -> Gemstones.
===============================================================

DONE
